00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.780
And now, back to the show.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.110
Let's start with the first
episode of the season,

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.200
in which the kids understandably

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.570
just really wanna wear pajamas to school.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.940
But this being "South Park" in 2022,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.670
Matt and Trey also found some time

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.340
to rag on celebrity love
for decentralized currency.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.110
Let's check out our first clip.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.711
- Bruh! We can have some goddamn balls!

00:03:30.711 --> 00:03:32.580
What does Matt Damon say
on that Bitcoin commercial?

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.190
Fortune favors the brave.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.620
- My dad said he listened to Matt Damon

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:36.820
and lost all his money.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:37.930
- Yes, everyone did!

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.680
- Okay, so it's worth
quickly noting the continuity

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.680
between this nod to
celebrity crypto endorsements

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.960
and the NFT plotline in the
"South Park" post-COVID special,

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.700
which we previously covered.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.720
It's a nice hint of things to come,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.560
as it's clear that for Matt and Trey,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.100
the hype around crypto
simply represents one facet

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.230
of the utter emptiness at
the heart of our culture.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.460
And okay, until a crypto bro
comes knocking at my door

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.810
with an exciting spiritual opportunity,

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.580
crypto isn't technically a religion.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:11.500
But it does make me think
of Marx's infamous assertion

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.810
that "religion is the
opiate of the masses."

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.900
This line is up there with
Nietzsche's "God is dead"

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.980
in the hall of fame of
philosophical statements

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.900
that are oft quoted and rarely understood.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.480
And that's because people,
they always ignore the words

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.560
that immediately precede Marx's assertion

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.450
that religion is the name of a new pill

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.400
produced by the Sackler family,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.890
'cause the Sacklers are bad people

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:35.740
that make opiates that
are ruining our country.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.520
Those words are, "Religion is the sigh

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.890
of the oppressed creature,

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:41.990
the heart of a heartless world,

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.860
and the soul of soulless conditions,"

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.940
meaning that Marx isn't some
religion-hating edgelord.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.490
Instead, he's pointing out
that when people's lives

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.540
and social conditions suck
ass, they look for meaning

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.250
and purpose somewhere else.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.410
And this is a theme
throughout these episodes:

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.560
folks trying to find meaning and purpose

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.570
in their otherwise-meaningless lives,

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.890
except they don't do it through religion.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.860
They do it in much dumber ways.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:07.940
- Hey, guys?

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.770
I just wanna say I'm really proud

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:11.660
to be a part of this office.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.170
Wearing our pajamas shows

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.680
that we care about those schoolkids,

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:17.810
that we care about other people.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.920
South Park realtors!

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.680
- [All] South Park realtors!

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.770
- Okay, so this idea that
wearing pajamas shows

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.630
that you care about kids, or
more generally that wearing

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.130
or purchasing a certain product signifies

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:35.510
you showing solidarity
with a marginalized group,

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.470
is an empty gesture at best,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.550
and it shows how our soulless condition

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.680
is no longer centered around religion,

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.710
but some mix of marketing and capitalism.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:46.880
Maybe it used to be important

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.490
to define yourself via
a religious affiliation,

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:54.090
i.e., "As a good Catholic, I
would never have premarital sex

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.740
during Lent or on Christmas."

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.210
But these days, it's more
about defining ourselves

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.230
via our consumer habits,

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.670
i.e., "I wear pajamas to
stand with the children"

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:08.380
or "I eat at IHOP because they
only serve pajama people."

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.530
- I'm sorry, sir, but
we aren't seating anyone

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.820
who isn't wearing their pajamas.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.340
- And this transition from
religion to capitalism,

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:17.640
or more accurately capitalism

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.890
becoming the dominant system of belief,

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.180
has turned lots of social
and political issues

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.110
into matters of consumption.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.580
As Walter Benjamin wrote

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.547
in the aptly titled
"Capitalism as Religion,"

00:06:28.547 --> 00:06:31.220
"A religion may be
discerned in capitalism.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.910
That is to say, capitalism
serves essentially

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:37.950
to allay the same anxieties,
torments, and disturbances

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.890
to which the so-called
religions offered answers."

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.040
- Since the legalization of marijuana,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.360
communities of color,
Black and Brown Coloradans,

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.170
those most affected by
the racist war on drugs,

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.340
have now been locked out
of the wealth creation

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:52.544
of the industry.

00:06:52.544 --> 00:06:55.600
(Randy yawns)
(inspiring music)

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.120
Luckily, the public is starting

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.030
to understand this unfairness,

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:00.420
and many people are now talking

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.333
of boycotting cannabis growers
who are only white-owned.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.410
- Boycotting? Wait, what?

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.610
- We are seeing a healthy
and dramatic spike

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.990
in consumers who demand that
their marijuana be grown

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:15.248
by those who understand the
fight for social equity.

00:07:15.248 --> 00:07:16.600
- Huh, huh, huh?

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.270
- The bottom line is this:

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.180
a completely white-owned
weed business these days

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.049
just isn't going to survive.

00:07:23.049 --> 00:07:24.210
(dramatic music)

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.860
- Wow, what's he gonna do? Okay.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.140
Let's get back to that fun word

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.650
we talked about before, neoliberalism.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:34.580
Now, it's a slippery term to define

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:37.960
only because it's a bit like
explaining water to a fish,

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.110
except we're the fish

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:44.110
and neoliberalism is
basically everything else.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.270
It's a political and economic ideology

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.380
that slowly wrapped its tentacles

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.920
around everything in our cultural life.

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.967
Academic David Harvey
described it like this:

00:07:53.967 --> 00:07:56.640
"Neoliberalism is in the
first instance a theory

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.220
of political and economic
practices that proposes

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.580
that human well-being can best be advanced

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:04.420
by liberating individual
entrepreneurial freedoms

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.900
and skills within an
institutional framework

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.450
characterized by strong
private property rights,

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.110
free markets, and free trade."

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:14.460
Basically, neoliberals argue
that we are most likely

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.000
to flourish under free
market economic conditions

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.100
with limited state control,
an unrestrained market,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.280
and strong individual
rights, which, I don't know,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.710
maybe doesn't sound so bad.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:31.710
But Harvey also argues that
neoliberalism is a class project

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:34.060
which is carried out by
and serves the interest

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:37.160
of a corporate capitalist class.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.680
Even notably rich Catholic
daddy Pope Francis has referred

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:44.580
to what he calls the
dogma of neoliberal faith

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:45.750
to describe the system.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:47.230
It's almost like it's a system

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.310
that people can put their faith into

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:51.930
due to the promise of a better life

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:56.170
but at its core is just
serving a small cadre of bros,

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.620
kind of like cryptocurrency?

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:03.390
And you might be thinking,
"But sir, I am no neoliberal.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.190
I completely get how this is a system

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:06.930
that serves the interest of one tiny group

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.060
at the expense of the rest of us.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:10.580
How dare you lump me in with it?"

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:12.680
But according to many scholars,

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.750
that's the wild thing about neoliberalism.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:17.910
Its brilliance is its
ability to affect everything

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.230
in our culture, not just
economic and political systems.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:25.050
In his book "Neoliberalism's
Demons," Adam Kotsko argues

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:29.250
that neoliberalism has
become a complete way of life

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.130
with a holistic worldview.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.320
He even goes so far as
to look at neoliberalism

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:35.140
as a political theology,

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:37.390
again following Marx and Benjamin's lead.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.300
And this holistic worldview

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.600
has penetrated the town of South Park.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:44.820
This character is explaining
that the very real plight

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.440
of marginalized communities
under the war on drugs

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.540
is being addressed not through,
say, widespread exonerations

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:54.920
or by broadening economic
and social support systems

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:58.110
for folks who went to jail
for doing or selling weed.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:01.660
Instead, the racism inherent
in America's drug policies

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:05.440
and weed industry can be
solved by spending your money

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.060
at the right businesses and
boycotting the wrong ones.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:11.730
It's neoliberalism to a T.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:13.940
And of course, we go on to see Randy try

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.130
to turn these systemic injustices

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.200
into a clever marketing
opportunity for his own weed brand.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.120
- Actions speak louder than words, gang.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.771
I guess, I guess I just
want you to think about it.

00:10:26.771 --> 00:10:28.270
(heartfelt music)

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:30.970
- Wow. Dad got woke.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.640
This right here makes me think

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:34.927
of one of my favorite lines
from Soren Kierkegaard,

00:10:34.927 --> 00:10:38.410
and this comes from a short
essay called "The Present Age."

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:39.243
And he says this.

00:10:39.243 --> 00:10:43.170
He says, "A revolutionary
age is an age of action.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.770
Ours is the age of
advertisement and publicity."

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.030
It works as a description

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.370
of the social effects of neoliberalism,

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:53.220
in which the economic
system can absorb all types

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.140
of calls for change in
various social movements

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:58.100
without actually changing the system

00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.650
that creates those problems,

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:02.910
i.e., neoliberalism will poison you

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:04.400
and sell you the antidote.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.860
And this is very much
what Randy is up to here,

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.190
in which his response
to the systemic racism

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:11.840
at the heart of the weed
industry is to get a picture

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:14.740
with Tolkien's dad and
throw it up on a billboard.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:17.350
And this isn't "South Park"
saying anything extreme.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:19.400
Our current president once argued

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.070
that seeing interracial couples

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.380
in commercials from
major brands was evidence

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:25.710
that our country was less racist,

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:28.090
rather than, you know,
simply proof that marketing

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:31.100
to interracial couples is an
economically promising prospect

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.650
for big corporations.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.020
For Kierkegaard, this comes
down to the distinction

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.830
between action and reflection

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:41.700
or doing the right things and
thinking the right things.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.420
This emphasis on belief over action

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.760
complements Marx and Benjamin's ideas

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.300
about capitalism becoming
a type of religion

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.220
in which believing the right
things is more important

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:55.300
than changing the world
in any significant way.

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:58.710
It's the difference between
action and advertising.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:01.500
Randy isn't reflecting on
inequality in the weed industry

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.960
to think critically about
his complicity in the system

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.420
and what actions could change it.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:08.030
He just wants to feel
good by publicly agreeing

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.030
with the right ideas.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:11.970
Like pajama-wearing real estate agents

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:13.880
performing solidarity with the kids,

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.400
Randy's performing anti-racism

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.730
for his own economic interests.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:20.700
Okay, so let's check this out.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:25.370
Stan went to the doctor because
he had symptoms of racism

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:28.030
and he wants to see if
the doctor can fix him.

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:30.530
- You really thought a couple
of Black people had a child

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:32.550
and named him Token?

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:35.090
Why would anyone name a Black kid Token?

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:36.480
You're a piece of (beep).

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:37.313
- No, I was just trying-

00:12:37.313 --> 00:12:39.440
- You were just going along
with the dominant culture

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:40.570
of the white paradigm.

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:41.490
That's what you were just...

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:42.330
Get out of my office!

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:43.640
You make me sick.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:45.140
- I'm sorry.

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.230
- I don't know what's
wrong with some people.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:48.480
I wonder if anyone else thought

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:50.750
that this kid's name was Token.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:52.610
Huh? Anybody?

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:56.150
Anyone else just assume
his name was Token?

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:57.337
Because that's disgusting-
- He's looking at us.

00:12:57.337 --> 00:12:58.763
- and you are the problem.

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:00.980
- Doctor?
- He means us.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:02.850
- I thought I told you
to get out of my office

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:04.610
because you seriously make me sick.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.650
I'm gonna (beep) my
(beep) pants right now.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:08.500
- Doctor, please, I
don't wanna be like this.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:09.333
I'll do anything.

00:13:09.333 --> 00:13:10.190
Just tell me what to do.

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:11.670
- Well, you wanna change?

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.480
Then I suggest you start
doing a lot of reading.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.173
- Wow, the cure for racism is reading,

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:20.380
struggling to grapple with
the history of your racism

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.690
and your complicity in that history!

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:23.660
Read books.

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:27.510
Because this way, you can both
get access to the right ideas

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:31.350
while also buying more
stuff on amazon.com.

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:32.730
Thanks, Jeffrey Bezos.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:35.920
Now, think back to that
Kierkegaard quote I just mentioned,

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.410
where rather than passion and action,

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:40.800
an age of reflection boils down

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:43.980
to advertising and publicity,
where critical thought

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.990
and social justice become
marketing campaigns,

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:50.580
where Burger King reminds
us that Black lives matter.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.947
In the same essay, Kierkegaard says,

00:13:52.947 --> 00:13:56.090
"An age without passion has no values,

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.600
and everything is transformed
into representational ideas.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.920
There are certain remarks
and expressions which,

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.240
though true and reasonable
up to a point, are lifeless."

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:08.470
When a society has no real values,

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:11.070
even statements and ideas
that are true and valid

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:12.810
end up becoming pretty empty.

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.930
That about sums up this season's
thesis statement so far.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:19.110
And while Kierkegaard predates
the neoliberal revolution,

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.020
this is what many argue
neoliberalism does.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:25.780
It takes statements and
ideas that could be true,

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:27.750
sucks out their political force,

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:30.110
turns them into easily marketed slogans,

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:32.910
and sells them back to us as a product.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:36.930
Real values are replaced by
toothless cultural signifiers.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:38.270
- One of our fourth-grade students says

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.240
he's done a lot of work
and has grown as a person,

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:41.690
and he'd like to share
his journey with you.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:42.990
Please welcome Stan Marsh.

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.580
- 'Sup, guys?

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:47.660
You know, I've done a
lot of reading lately,

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.930
and I'd like to announce that
the school has allowed me

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:53.814
to declare today J. R. R.
Tolkien Appreciation Day.

00:14:53.814 --> 00:14:56.280
- Look at this little poser
with his little poser glasses.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:00.610
Okay, now, to be clear,
reading books is good.

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:01.530
We love books.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.260
Philosophers especially love books.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:08.230
But there's reason to be
skeptical of Stan's journey,

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.100
beyond his obnoxious
pair of poser spectacles.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.510
Now, Nietzsche said that
"the oversaturation of an age

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:17.800
with history seems to be
hostile and dangerous to life"

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.890
because it can "lead an
age into a dangerous mood

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:23.550
of irony in regard to
itself and subsequently

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.850
into the more dangerous mood of cynicism,"

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:29.400
a mood "through which the
forces of life are paralyzed

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:30.960
and at last destroyed."

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.750
Now, here, it seems
like the show is arguing

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:34.860
for something similar.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:38.270
Now, it's pretty ironic when
neoliberal turns movements

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:41.440
about fundamentally changing
the world into excuses

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:45.460
to buy books, listen to
podcasts, or wear cool T-shirts.

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:47.930
And this turns from irony into cynicism

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:49.920
when people slowly
start to view everything

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.200
as just another marketing ploy

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.420
and just stop giving a (beep) altogether.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.840
In reading "The Lord of the Rings"

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:58.240
to understand his friend Tolkien,

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.060
Stan just ends up making
things about himself

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:04.410
and hasn't done anything to
make Tolkien's life any better.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.020
He's performing a new
type of social identity.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.640
Creating a Tolkien holiday at
the school is a cynical ploy.

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:13.840
It's Stan's way of performing allyship

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:15.950
without actually changing
anything fundamental

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:18.640
about their school, town, or world.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:23.210
Okay, so enough with symbolic pajamas and,

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:26.570
you know, weed companies
doing marketing and books.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.660
Let's get to an industry with integrity,

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:32.640
purpose, and passion: real estate.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.170
So this is episode three.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:35.830
Let's cut over here.

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:38.120
There's a real estate craze
taking over South Park,

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:40.040
and Cartman wants to cash in.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.950
- Ever heard of a real
estate agent, Butters?

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:42.783
- Well, not really.

00:16:42.783 --> 00:16:44.310
- Yeah, well, neither had I.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.600
So I looked it up on a bunch
of those shows and stuff.

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:48.540
You don't do anything.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:50.010
You just look nice and hug people,

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.210
and then when someone buys or
sells a house, you make money.

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:54.040
- Wow. Well, that sounds like a good deal.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.250
- It's more than a good deal, Butters.

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:56.720
It's (beep) legal theft.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:01.130
- Okay, we're not doing a
"Real Estate Agent Reacts"

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:03.450
or anything here, but it's worth noting

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.030
that under neoliberalism,

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:08.480
rates of homeownership have plummeted,

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.270
and housing has turned into a commodity

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:12.810
with exponentially rising values,

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.550
values that sometimes seem
disconnected from the reality

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:18.140
of housing as a thing that humans need.

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.030
In the past year or so, there
have been multiple stories

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:22.170
about how more and more
single-family homes

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.620
are being purchased by Wall
Street investment firms

00:17:24.620 --> 00:17:26.160
or Silicon Valley investors.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.540
These groups grabbed
15% of available homes

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:30.320
in the first quarter of 2021.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:32.820
That's not counting all the
homes purchased to be turned

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:36.480
into Airbnbs and other
short-term rental investments.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:39.440
Cartman sees that something
about this system smells

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:41.150
like bull (beep), but
more than bull (beep),

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:42.850
he smells an opportunity

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.730
to exploit the bull (beep) for cash.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:50.240
I can almost hear the crowds
chanting "USA! USA!" right now,

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:51.920
because creating an industry

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:53.820
around what should be a human right

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:57.150
and then making wild
profits off speculating

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:00.974
on that thing is very much
in neoliberalism's Q-zone.

00:18:00.974 --> 00:18:03.410
- It doesn't work that way, Eric.

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.660
You aren't going to make any money.

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:06.787
You're just driving up the market,

00:18:06.787 --> 00:18:10.460
and none of the deals will
make it through escrow.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:12.460
- Well, we'll just see
in 30 days, won't we?

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:13.420
Except for some of my deals,

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:15.810
which have a 45-day contingency period!

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:16.870
- Okay, that can't be real,

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:19.170
'cause all the real
estate shows on TV tell me

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:22.500
that you make lots of
money and it's super easy.

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.020
Real estate agents, hit us in
the comments and let us know.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:28.140
Now, Cartman is operating
under the conditions

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:32.300
of neoliberal reality, the
one that sells us on the idea

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:34.360
that homeownership is
the most important thing

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:35.600
we can do with our money,

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:38.293
the one that pumps out more
real estate reality shows

00:18:38.293 --> 00:18:39.550
than we have time to watch,

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:41.650
and the one that tries to convince us

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:45.260
that a housing crisis caused
by unaffordable housing

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:48.700
can be solved by building more housing

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.270
for real estate agents to sell

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.150
and for Wall Street firms to buy.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.360
Now, Cartman's mom is aware
that much of the system

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.830
is just a house of cards
waiting to fall down,

00:18:56.830 --> 00:18:58.767
but she's still complicit within it,

00:18:58.767 --> 00:19:00.130
and we can understand this

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:03.240
via French psychoanalyst Jacques Lacan,

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.370
who was very familiar with
folks like Kierkegaard,

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:07.500
Nietzsche, and Marx that
we've discussed so far.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.640
He's also the Obi-Wan Kenobi

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:12.240
to Slavoj Zizek's Luke Skywalker.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.000
Now, Lacan drew a
distinction between reality,

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.110
which is the product of all the symbols

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:19.430
and structures that make
up our social world,

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:23.200
and the real, which is all
the messy stuff that exists

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.210
on the other side of those
symbols and structures

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:28.490
and is in constant tension with reality.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:29.500
In other words,

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:33.550
the real is what any
reality has to suppress,

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:37.770
and reality is constituted
via this act of repression.

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.410
It's a mind (beep) of a concept,

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:42.780
and Lacan himself changed its
meaning throughout his life,

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:45.330
but the point here is that neoliberalism,

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.540
a system pretty accurately
represented in these episodes,

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:52.900
creates a reality that
tries to cover up the real.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:54.960
And in this season, at least so far,

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.770
Trey and Matt are pushing
some of the holy grails

00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.380
of neoliberalism to the point

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.990
where we can see the
absurdity of this reality.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:04.780
And while the show isn't
offering any magical solutions

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:07.770
to the neoliberal condition,
David Harvey has said

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:10.060
that "resistance to
neoliberalism can occur

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:11.510
in a number of different ways.

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:12.910
In my work I stress that the point

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:16.120
at which value is realized
is also a point of tension.

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:18.250
Value is produced in the labor process,

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:21.430
and this is a very important
aspect of class struggle.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:24.800
But value is realized in
the market through sale,

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.340
and there's a lot of politics to that.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:28.930
A lot of resistance to
capital accumulation occurs

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.860
not only on the point of production

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:34.490
but also through consumption
and the realization of value."

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.630
So basically, one way of
resistance is at the point

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:40.330
at which we make stuff, i.e., work.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:41.680
But there's also an opportunity

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:43.470
for resistance in consumption,

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:46.420
meaning that we have the
ability to call bull (beep)

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.050
on some of the products and ideas

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:51.750
that neoliberalism sells to
us as valuable and important.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:54.870
Okay, guys, I know I threw
kind of a lot at you today,

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:57.370
but what else do you expect
when they set me loose

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:59.980
on 60 minutes of adult
animation with a stack of books

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.540
and a fresh bag of coffee beans?

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:03.410
And if this all seems complicated,

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:06.870
it's because neoliberalism
is incredibly complex

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:09.030
and requires all the tools of philosophy,

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:11.480
theology, economics,
and even psychoanalysis

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:13.300
to really understand the system

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:15.380
and what the system is doing to us.

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:19.180
And because Matt and Trey
are cranky Gen X libertarians

00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:21.860
and not a couple of
wine-soaked French Marxists,

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:25.290
they offer no underlying message
for how the world should be

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.110
in these episodes, and that's okay,

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:29.260
because these episodes
still make it possible

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:31.510
to look at some
philosophical ideas that run

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:34.090
right through the 19th,
20th, and 21st centuries.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:35.150
And if you wanna dig deeper,

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:36.980
I'll leave you a reading
list in the description,

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:38.370
so check those out.

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.070
Now, thanks again for hanging out with us,

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:41.870
and please jump in the comments

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:43.220
and let us know what you think

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:44.830
about the ideas being explored

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:46.160
in this season of "South Park."

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:47.990
A huge thanks as always to our patrons

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:49.310
for supporting our channel,

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:52.490
and be sure to check out
Wisecrack's podcast as well.

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:56.060
Hit that subscribe button like
you're ringing a massive bell

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:59.810
that lets the world know that
you think all the right things

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:03.010
and don't forget to ring
that actual bell, either.

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:05.360
As always, let us know if
there's any other content

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:08.070
that you think warrants a
philosophical once-over.

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:11.145
As always, thanks for watching. Later.

00:22:11.145 --> 00:22:13.728
(upbeat music)

00:22:15.192 --> 00:22:19.010
I would never have
premarital sex during Lent

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:22.060
or on Christmas,

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:24.100
'cause it's just a hot day.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:25.280
You're under the mistletoe.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:27.340
You've had a few eggnogs.

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:28.360
Mom and Dad are asleep.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:31.170
You know, it's a good day for that.

